WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.480
Hi Reef. Welcome to Summit One.

00:00:03.020 --> 00:00:06.320
Get curious, discover clever ideas, and become a more

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:07.880
confident problem solver.

00:00:08.620 --> 00:00:10.680
Your adventure starts with one small step.

00:00:11.628 --> 00:00:14.988
You have three exciting destinations: the Australian

00:00:14.988 --> 00:00:19.748
Mathematics Competition, M A W A's Problem Solving Program,

00:00:20.428 --> 00:00:23.288
and Rossmoyne's Honours Mathematics programme.

00:00:23.288 --> 00:00:25.608
Each opens a different door into maths.

00:00:26.776 --> 00:00:30.556
Did you know the AMC began in nineteen seventy-eight?

00:00:30.556 --> 00:00:34.256
Primary divisions joined in two thousand and two.

00:00:34.256 --> 00:00:37.016
You're joining a long tradition of curious thinkers.

00:00:38.141 --> 00:00:42.141
A tricky-looking question can hide a wonderfully simple idea.

00:00:42.821 --> 00:00:45.481
You'll learn to look beneath the story and notice its

00:00:45.481 --> 00:00:46.621
mathematical structure.

00:00:47.572 --> 00:00:49.232
Watch this. One dot.

00:00:49.752 --> 00:00:51.352
Add three, then five, then seven.

00:00:51.752 --> 00:00:53.292
A four-by-four square appears.

00:00:54.392 --> 00:00:55.972
Suddenly, a sum becomes a picture.

00:00:57.244 --> 00:01:01.984
M A W A is the Mathematical Association of Western Australia.

00:01:02.824 --> 00:01:06.864
Its Problem Solving Program began in nineteen ninety-two.

00:01:06.864 --> 00:01:09.284
Explaining why something works matters here.

00:01:10.169 --> 00:01:13.709
You'll investigate patterns, test small cases, and build

00:01:13.709 --> 00:01:18.629
convincing explanations. Asking, 'Does this always work?' is

00:01:18.629 --> 00:01:20.649
where a mathematician's adventure begins.

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:25.590
Rossmoyne's Honours programme explores maths beyond the usual

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:29.170
curriculum. Did you know its students form the Fermat Club?

00:01:29.810 --> 00:01:32.310
They get together to solve problems and have fun.

00:01:33.282 --> 00:01:36.142
You'll build number sense, visual thinking, and flexible

00:01:36.142 --> 00:01:38.222
reasoning. Draw a diagram.

00:01:38.762 --> 00:01:42.202
Work backwards. Break a big puzzle into smaller pieces.

00:01:43.204 --> 00:01:47.244
Each lesson introduces an idea, shows how it works, then helps

00:01:47.244 --> 00:01:48.004
you try it.

00:01:48.644 --> 00:01:51.544
Quick choices warm up your thinking before deeper challenges.

00:01:52.677 --> 00:01:54.997
Then you'll tackle unfamiliar problems.

00:01:55.837 --> 00:01:59.277
You'll practise recognising a useful method, even when the

00:01:59.277 --> 00:02:00.997
numbers and the story have changed.

00:02:01.924 --> 00:02:03.584
Getting stuck is part of the work.

00:02:04.244 --> 00:02:05.384
Try a smaller example.

00:02:05.804 --> 00:02:06.324
Make a sketch.

00:02:07.264 --> 00:02:07.664
Check a clue.

00:02:08.484 --> 00:02:10.664
Every careful attempt gives you something to learn.

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:15.460
Practice papers let you rehearse: read carefully, choose a

00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:18.260
strategy, manage your time, and check your answers.

00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:21.740
Afterwards, use the worked solutions to improve.

00:02:22.949 --> 00:02:24.869
When exam day arrives, take a breath.

00:02:25.649 --> 00:02:26.409
Start with what you know.

00:02:26.929 --> 00:02:29.329
You can move on and return to a tough question.

00:02:30.069 --> 00:02:32.509
Aim high, one thoughtful step at a time.

00:02:33.556 --> 00:02:35.536
You don't need all the answers today, Reef.

00:02:36.296 --> 00:02:36.936
Bring your curiosity.

00:02:37.896 --> 00:02:38.656
Build your toolkit.

00:02:39.276 --> 00:02:40.716
Enjoy those aha moments.

00:02:41.396 --> 00:02:43.016
Your next discovery starts here.

